CLOSEOUT GATE: two stray untracked files in realisateur block EVERY subagent closeout, and one of them is an unread human directive.

Found 2026-08-11 by a subagent whose own work was in hf7y/scheduler (PR #95); subagent-closeout.sh FLAGged realisateur dirty-tree for files that run never touched.

1. `.claude/settings.json` (untracked, mtime 2026-08-10 20:39, NEVER tracked in any branch). A real permissions allowlist. Every subagent stopping in this repo now hits `FLAG [dirty-tree]` for it and must either adopt a config file it did not write, delete it, or argue past the gate. Decide once: track it, or add it to .gitignore. Note .gitignore already ignores `.claude/worktrees/` while `.claude/commands/*` IS tracked, so "everything under .claude is ignored" is not the existing rule.

2. `260807-zach-reply.txt` (untracked, mtime 2026-08-07, never tracked) is a DIRECT HUMAN DIRECTIVE sitting unread for 4 days at the repo root, and it bears on live work. Verbatim: "We must retire the blockers file as soon as possible. sweep-loop-common.sh must be retired as well. This is urgent." Also "vim-arcade needs its deploy key fixed. And we need to diagnose how this has taken so long to fix." Also "ensure that sonde is now what we use rather than another ecosim sensor. write a test that outputs whether things are properly wired up."

Why this matters right now: scheduler PR #95 (today) MODIFIED lib/sweep-loop-common.sh to fix the consume/pull deadlock (scheduler#61/#70) without knowing this directive existed -- it says retire that file, urgently. The fix is still correct and still needed (the deadlock is not BLOCKERS-specific; .scheduler/QUESTIONS.md consumes the same way), but the retirement is a bigger call nobody has made. Same directive appears as the 2026-08-07 comment on scheduler#61: "Just get rid of the entire BLOCKERS architecture."

This is the buried-finding pattern again: a correct human answer sitting in the tree while agents re-derive around it. A human directive should not live as an untracked file at a repo root -- it is invisible to every reader except a dirty-tree gate.

NOT touched by that run: neither file was created, edited, or deleted by it. Deliberately left as found -- committing another writer .claude config or deleting a 4-day-old human reply are both worse than reporting them.
